  <h1>ML Algorithms Used in Trading</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Why this matters for <strong>HFT_practice</strong>: three different learners are
    already in this repo. <code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> uses linear
    regression. <code>fpga/moving_average_trader.vhd</code> uses a rule (technically not
    ML — and that matters). <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code> uses
    <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> — the project's flagship model.
    You should know what each one actually <em>does</em> and why each one lives where it does.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §1 BIG IDEA ===================== -->
  <h2>1. What does "ML for trading" actually mean in this repo?</h2>

  <p>
    Every supervised ML model is a function <code>ŷ = f(x; θ)</code> learned from
    <em>(input, output)</em> pairs. The art is choosing <code>x</code>, <code>y</code>,
    and the shape of <code>f</code>. This repo's choice:
  </p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Variable</th><th>In this project</th><th>Where it's set</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>x</code> (features)</td>
      <td>per-day, per-ticker: <code>close</code>, <code>ret_1d</code>, <code>ma_5</code>, <code>ma_10</code>, <code>ma_gap</code>, <code>vol_10</code>, <code>rsi_14</code></td>
      <td><code>build_features</code> in <code>paper_trading_sim.py</code></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>y</code> (target)</td>
      <td><strong>Next day's return</strong>, <code>fwd_ret_1d = close.pct_change().shift(-1)</code></td>
      <td>same file, line 68</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>f</code> (model)</td>
      <td><code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> from scikit-learn</td>
      <td><code>train_model</code>, line 113</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>θ</code> (parameters)</td>
      <td>The internal weights/tree structure learned from history</td>
      <td>Saved to <code>models/*.joblib</code></td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>The single most consequential design choice in the repo:</strong>
    predicting a <em>continuous</em> next-day return (regression), not a
    <em>direction</em> (up/down classification), not an <em>absolute price</em>.
    This is why the file is named <code>GradientBoosting<u>Regressor</u></code> and
    not <code>...Classifier</code>. The continuous predictions are then
    <em>sorted</em>, and the top-N become the portfolio.
  </div>

  <h3>The pipeline, from data to dollars</h3>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">1</span>
    <div><strong>Raw data:</strong> daily OHLCV from yfinance for every ticker in the universe.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">▼</div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">2</span>
    <div><strong>Features:</strong> <code>build_features</code> turns prices into returns, moving averages, an MA gap, 10-day vol, RSI. 7 numbers per (ticker, date).</div>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">▼</div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">3</span>
    <div><strong>Target:</strong> next day's return is shifted into the same row. Training pairs (x, y) ready.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">▼</div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">4</span>
    <div><strong>Model:</strong> a single <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> is fit on the <em>stacked</em> data from all tickers (one model, not one-per-ticker).</div>
  </div>
  <div class="arrow">▼</div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">5</span>
    <div><strong>Rank &amp; allocate:</strong> for each day, predict next-day returns for every ticker, sort, take <code>top_n</code>, send to <code>meanvar_long_only</code> for risk-aware weights.</div>
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §2 THREE ALGORITHMS ===================== -->
  <h2>2. The three algorithms in this repo, side by side</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Linear Regression — small, fast, FPGA-friendly</h4>
      <p><code>ŷ = w₀ + w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ</code></p>
      <p>Just a weighted sum. <strong>Parametric</strong> (fixed number of parameters regardless of dataset size). Trained by closed-form least squares.</p>
      <p>Lives in <code>fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd</code> and the C++ at <code>gradient_boosting/sample_with_gradient_boosting.cpp</code> (despite the filename).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Moving Average Crossover — a rule, not ML</h4>
      <p>Buy when price &lt; (MA − spread). Sell when price &gt; (MA + spread).</p>
      <p>Zero learned parameters. <strong>This is not machine learning</strong> — it's a hand-coded mean-reversion rule. Useful baseline to beat.</p>
      <p>Lives in <code>fpga/moving_average_trader.vhd</code>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Gradient Boosting Regressor — flagship</h4>
      <p>An ensemble of ~100 small decision trees, where each tree corrects the errors of the previous ones. <strong>Non-parametric</strong>: model size grows with the data and complexity of the signal.</p>
      <p>Lives in <code>paper_trading_sim.py:train_model</code>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Why three? They serve different masters</h4>
      <p>Linear: tiny LUT footprint → FPGA viable. Moving average: zero training data needed → instant baseline. Gradient boosting: best predictive power → wins on the Sharpe ratio, but too big for one chip cycle.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>What each one can express</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Model</th><th>Can capture</th><th>Cannot capture</th><th>Training data needed</th></tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Moving avg rule</td>
      <td>Mean reversion at one timescale</td>
      <td>Anything else</td>
      <td>None</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Linear regression</td>
      <td>Monotonic, additive effects per feature</td>
      <td>Interactions, non-linearities</td>
      <td>Hundreds of rows</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Gradient boosting</td>
      <td>Non-linear effects, feature interactions, thresholds</td>
      <td>Very long-range temporal dependencies (use RNN/transformer for that)</td>
      <td>Thousands to millions of rows</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <!-- ===================== §3 BOOSTING DETAIL ===================== -->
  <h2>3. What <code>GradientBoostingRegressor</code> actually does</h2>

  <p>
    The name confuses people. Break it down:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"Regressor"</strong> = predicts a continuous number (next-day return), not a class.</li>
    <li><strong>"Gradient"</strong> = each new tree is trained to predict the <em>gradient of the loss</em> — i.e., the direction the current prediction needs to move. For squared-error loss, that's just the residual.</li>
    <li><strong>"Boosting"</strong> = combine many weak learners (shallow trees) sequentially, each one fixing what the previous ensemble got wrong.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Step by step, for the squared-error loss the regressor uses by default:</p>

  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">1</span>
    <div><strong>Initialize</strong> the ensemble prediction at the mean of <code>y</code>: <code>F₀(x) = mean(y_train)</code>.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">2</span>
    <div><strong>Compute residuals</strong> for every training row: <code>rᵢ = yᵢ − F(xᵢ)</code>. These are how far off the current ensemble is on each row.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">3</span>
    <div><strong>Fit a small tree</strong> to predict the residuals (depth typically 3–8). The tree carves the feature space into rectangles and predicts a constant residual in each rectangle.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">4</span>
    <div><strong>Add the tree</strong> to the ensemble, scaled by a small learning rate (<code>ν</code>, e.g. 0.1):
    <code>F_new(x) = F(x) + ν · tree(x)</code>.</div>
  </div>
  <div class="boost-step">
    <span class="num">5</span>
    <div><strong>Repeat</strong> from step 2 until you have <code>n_estimators</code> trees (default 100).</div>
  </div>

  <p class="small">
    Final prediction: <code>F(x) = mean(y) + ν · (tree₁(x) + tree₂(x) + … + tree₁₀₀(x))</code>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Why this works for trading returns: each individual tree is too weak to overfit, but
    the ensemble can compose hundreds of small, non-linear "this feature crosses that
    threshold AND that other feature is high" rules — exactly the kind of regime-specific
    signal that a single linear weight can't express.
  </p>

  <h3>Tiny, runnable example</h3>
<pre><span class="kw">from</span> sklearn.ensemble <span class="kw">import</span> GradientBoostingRegressor
<span class="kw">import</span> numpy <span class="kw">as</span> np

<span class="com"># Toy: 200 days, 2 features (e.g., today's return and 10-day vol)</span>
rng = np.random.default_rng(<span class="num">0</span>)
X = rng.normal(size=(<span class="num">200</span>, <span class="num">2</span>))
<span class="com"># True signal: positive when feature 0 > 0 AND feature 1 < 0 (an interaction)</span>
y = (X[:, <span class="num">0</span>] > <span class="num">0</span>) * (X[:, <span class="num">1</span>] < <span class="num">0</span>) * <span class="num">0.02</span> + rng.normal(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">0.005</span>, <span class="num">200</span>)

model = <span class="fn">GradientBoostingRegressor</span>(n_estimators=<span class="num">100</span>, max_depth=<span class="num">3</span>, learning_rate=<span class="num">0.1</span>)
model.<span class="fn">fit</span>(X, y)

<span class="com"># Predict and inspect feature importances</span>
<span class="fn">print</span>(model.<span class="fn">predict</span>(X[:<span class="num">5</span>]))
<span class="fn">print</span>(model.feature_importances_)   <span class="com"># which feature contributes most to splits</span>
</pre>

  <p>
    Linear regression would <em>miss</em> the interaction signal in that toy entirely
    — it would only see "feature 0 has slight positive correlation" and shrug.
    Gradient boosting picks up the joint rule because depth-3 trees can split on both
    features at once.
  </p>

  <!-- ===================== §4 IN THIS PROJECT ===================== -->
  <h2>4. In this project</h2>

  <span class="filepath">paper_trading_sim.py (lines 48–69)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">build_features</span>(df: pd.DataFrame) -> pd.DataFrame:
    out = pd.DataFrame(index=df.index)
    out[<span class="str">"close"</span>]   = df[<span class="str">"Close"</span>]
    out[<span class="str">"ret_1d"</span>]  = out[<span class="str">"close"</span>].<span class="fn">pct_change</span>()
    out[<span class="str">"ma_5"</span>]    = out[<span class="str">"close"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(<span class="num">5</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()
    out[<span class="str">"ma_10"</span>]   = out[<span class="str">"close"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(<span class="num">10</span>).<span class="fn">mean</span>()
    out[<span class="str">"ma_gap"</span>]  = (out[<span class="str">"ma_5"</span>] / out[<span class="str">"ma_10"</span>]) - <span class="num">1</span>     <span class="com"># short-vs-long-MA</span>
    out[<span class="str">"vol_10"</span>]  = out[<span class="str">"ret_1d"</span>].<span class="fn">rolling</span>(<span class="num">10</span>).<span class="fn">std</span>()
    out[<span class="str">"rsi_14"</span>]  = <span class="fn">_rsi</span>(out[<span class="str">"close"</span>], <span class="num">14</span>) / <span class="num">100.0</span>      <span class="com"># normalized RSI</span>
    out[<span class="str">"fwd_ret_1d"</span>] = out[<span class="str">"ret_1d"</span>].<span class="fn">shift</span>(-<span class="num">1</span>)    <span class="com"># THE TARGET — note shift(-1)</span>
    <span class="kw">return</span> out.<span class="fn">dropna</span>()
</pre>

  <p>
    The <code>.shift(-1)</code> on the last line is doing the heavy semantic lifting:
    it puts <em>tomorrow's</em> return on <em>today's</em> row, so when you train,
    you're learning "given today's features → predict tomorrow's return." Get that
    direction wrong and you've built a look-ahead-biased model that looks great in
    backtests and loses money live.
  </p>

  <span class="filepath">paper_trading_sim.py (lines 113–125)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">train_model</span>(X: np.ndarray, y: np.ndarray) -> GradientBoostingRegressor:
    model = <span class="fn">GradientBoostingRegressor</span>(random_state=<span class="num">42</span>)
    model.<span class="fn">fit</span>(X, y)
    <span class="kw">return</span> model
</pre>

  <p>
    Notice what's <em>not</em> here: no hyperparameter tuning, no cross-validation, no
    regularization knobs explicitly set. sklearn's defaults are
    <code>n_estimators=100</code>, <code>max_depth=3</code>, <code>learning_rate=0.1</code>,
    <code>loss="squared_error"</code> — reasonable, conservative starting points for a
    learner this size. A real production pipeline would sweep these.
  </p>

  <span class="filepath">paper_trading_sim.py (lines 218–231) — what the prediction is used for</span>
<pre><span class="kw">for</span> dt, day_df <span class="kw">in</span> pred_df.<span class="fn">groupby</span>(level=<span class="num">0</span>):
    day_sorted = day_df.<span class="fn">sort_values</span>(<span class="str">"pred"</span>, ascending=<span class="kw">False</span>)
    picks = day_sorted.<span class="fn">head</span>(cfg.top_n)        <span class="com"># <-- rank, take top-N</span>
    weight = <span class="num">1.0</span> / <span class="fn">len</span>(picks)
    day_ret = <span class="fn">float</span>((picks[<span class="str">"fwd_ret_1d"</span>].<span class="fn">mean</span>()))   <span class="com"># equal-weight realized return</span>
</pre>

  <p>
    Crucial insight: the model's predicted <em>magnitude</em> barely matters. What
    matters is its <strong>cross-sectional ranking</strong> — which tickers it ranks
    highest today. A model that systematically underestimates returns but still ranks
    them in the right order will beat a model with low MAE but a noisy ranking.
  </p>

  <h3>And on the FPGA: linear regression instead</h3>
  <span class="filepath">fpga/linear_regression_predictor.vhd (lines 75–78)</span>
<pre><span class="kw">constant</span> W0   : signed(31 <span class="kw">downto</span> 0) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>(  50, 32);   <span class="com">--  0.05 ×1000 — intercept</span>
<span class="kw">constant</span> W_F0 : signed(31 <span class="kw">downto</span> 0) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>( 300, 32);   <span class="com">--  0.30 ×1000 — feature 0 weight</span>
<span class="kw">constant</span> W_F1 : signed(31 <span class="kw">downto</span> 0) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>(-100, 32);   <span class="com">-- -0.10 ×1000 — feature 1 weight</span>
<span class="kw">constant</span> W_F2 : signed(31 <span class="kw">downto</span> 0) := <span class="fn">to_signed</span>( 200, 32);   <span class="com">--  0.20 ×1000 — feature 2 weight</span>
</pre>
  <p>
    Four <em>numbers</em>. That's the entire model. This is what makes linear
    regression FPGA-viable: 4 constants → 3 multiplies + 3 adds → done in 2 clock
    cycles. The 100-tree gradient booster cannot collapse into that.
  </p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Repo honesty:</strong> the Python gradient boosting model is more accurate but
    can't run on the FPGA. The FPGA linear regression is fast but expresses only a tiny
    fraction of what the Python model learned. The honest bridge is what <code>fpga/README.md</code>
    points at: <strong>hls4ml</strong> or <strong>Vitis AI</strong>, which compile larger models
    into HDL by quantizing and pruning. None of that is wired up yet — it's the obvious next
    step for whoever takes this repo seriously.
  </div>

  <!-- ===================== §5 CONTRASTS ===================== -->
  <h2>5. Often confused with</h2>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Regression vs Classification</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Regression</strong> predicts a number on a continuous scale (this repo:
        next-day return as a decimal). <strong>Classification</strong> predicts a label
        (e.g., "up" or "down"). Trading-specific tradeoff: classification throws away
        magnitude information that is essential for ranking and sizing positions.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Gradient Boosting vs Random Forest</h4>
      <p>
        Both are tree ensembles. <strong>Random forest</strong> trains many <em>independent</em>
        deep trees on bootstrap samples and averages them — reduces variance.
        <strong>Gradient boosting</strong> trains <em>sequential</em> shallow trees, each
        correcting the previous — reduces bias. Boosting usually wins on tabular data; forests
        are more robust to bad hyperparameters.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Linear Regression vs Logistic Regression</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Linear regression</strong> outputs an unbounded number; <strong>logistic
        regression</strong> outputs a probability in (0, 1) via a sigmoid. Despite the
        name, logistic regression is a <em>classifier</em>. This repo uses linear, not
        logistic.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Parametric vs Non-parametric</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Parametric</strong> models have a fixed parameter count regardless of data
        size (linear regression: N weights). <strong>Non-parametric</strong> models grow
        with the data (gradient boosting: number and depth of trees scale).
        Why it matters here: parametric fits an FPGA; non-parametric usually doesn't.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Training vs Inference</h4>
      <p>
        <strong>Training</strong> is the expensive offline step that produces the parameters
        (<code>model.fit(X, y)</code>). <strong>Inference</strong> is the cheap online step
        that uses them (<code>model.predict(X)</code>). HFT cares about inference latency;
        the model lives or dies on it. Training latency essentially doesn't matter.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card">
      <h4>Feature vs Target</h4>
      <p>
        A <strong>feature</strong> is what the model sees as input on day T (RSI, MA gap,
        etc.). The <strong>target</strong> is what it's trying to predict — here, the return
        on day T+1. If you accidentally include something from T+1 in the features, you've
        invented a model that "predicts" what it already knows. That's
        <strong>look-ahead bias</strong> and it's the #1 way backtests lie to you.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

