  <!-- ===================== §7 SCENARIOS ===================== -->
  <h2>7. Check your understanding (scenarios)</h2>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S1. A teammate adds <code>out["high_today"] = df["High"]</code> to <code>build_features</code>. What's the problem?</summary>
    <p>
      Subtle but lethal: <code>df["High"]</code> is the day's high price, only known
      <em>after</em> the day closes. Targets are also "after the close" (next day's
      return). If today's row contains today's high (computed at end-of-day) and you
      use it to "predict" tomorrow's return, you'd be fine — BUT if you ever apply
      the model intraday for live trading, you don't yet know today's high, and the
      live features won't match the training distribution. The model will appear to
      work in backtest and fail live. Always ask: "would I actually know this feature
      at the moment I want to act?"
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S2. You sweep <code>n_estimators</code> from 100 → 500 in <code>train_model</code>, and the training R² goes from 0.05 to 0.40 while the test R² <em>drops</em>. What happened, and what would you adjust?</summary>
    <p>
      Classic <strong>overfitting</strong>: more trees means the ensemble can memorize
      noise in the training data. The fix is regularization — lower
      <code>learning_rate</code> (0.05 or 0.03), reduce <code>max_depth</code> (already 3
      by default — try 2), add <code>subsample=0.8</code> (stochastic gradient boosting),
      and use early stopping based on a held-out validation slice. In time-series
      specifically: <em>never</em> use random k-fold; use a chronological train/validation
      split or, better, walk-forward — which is exactly what <code>walkforward_sim.py</code>
      does for evaluation.
    </p>
  </details>

  <details class="scenario">
    <summary>S3. <code>predict_next.py</code> calls <code>meanvar_long_only(mu, Sigma, max_w)</code> on the top-N predictions. Is that step ML?</summary>
    <p>
      No — it's <strong>optimization</strong>, not learning. <code>mu</code> is the model's
      predicted expected returns, <code>Sigma</code> is the empirical covariance matrix of
      historical returns. The function solves a closed-form portfolio problem
      (Markowitz mean-variance with non-negativity + capping). Nothing is fit; nothing
      is learned. This is a critical mental separation: ML produces the <em>signals</em>
      (mu), portfolio optimization decides <em>how to act on them</em> (w).
    </p>
  </details>

